{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>\"And he shall pour and he shall put and he shall bring it\"</b> — this teaches that pouring and mixing are valid through a non-priest (zar). <b>\"The priests and he shall take a handful\"</b> — from the handful-taking (kemitzah) onward is a priestly service; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 2:1). But it requires clarification that the priestly service does not begin at the handful-taking, for the presentation (hagashah) precedes the handful-taking and it is invalid through a non-priest, as it says (Leviticus 2:8), \"And he shall bring it to the priest and he shall present it to the altar,\" and afterwards, \"And the priest shall raise up its memorial-portion\" (Leviticus 2:9), which is the handful-taking, for the priest is the one who presents it to the altar at the southwest corner and afterwards takes the handful. And thus we learned (Kiddushin 36a): leanings of the hands, wave-offerings, presentations, and handful-takings apply to men and do not apply to women. And they said: presentations, as it is written, \"This is the law of the meal-offering: the sons of Aaron shall present it\" (Leviticus 6:7) — and not the daughters of Aaron. If so, the presentation belongs to the sons of Aaron. But what they said (Menahot 9a), \"From the handful-taking onward is a priestly service\" — their intent is that from this handful-taking that is written in this verse onward is a priestly service, not regarding the matters that Scripture set forth here before the handful-taking. For in this passage it mentioned pouring, the placing of frankincense, the bringing to the priest, and the handful-taking, and the presentation to the altar was not mentioned here. It is therefore found that all the matters stated here before the handful-taking are valid through a non-priest — they are the pouring, the mixing, and the bringing."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:45.236006622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}